# The knowledge graph of the knowledge graph

The **knowledge graph** of this vault is what the cards are once their headers
and links are read as data: nodes of a few types, joined by arrows of a few
types. This card describes that graph. It is also a node of it, of the type
`meta`, and its header holds the schema that the rest of the graph is checked
against. The schema therefore has to allow for the card that states it.
The term and the design are proposed here and are not established usage.

## Description

### The graph

Every card is a node, typed by the `category` in its header. Every claim in a
header is a node as well, named by its address, such as `strange-loop#2`. A
name that appears in an atom and has no card is a node of the type `term`.

| Node type | What it is |
|---|---|
| `concept` | a card that defines one concept |
| `question` | a card that states one of the project's questions |
| `star` | a card that states an abstraction over claims of several concepts |
| `meta` | a card that describes the graph; this one |
| `index` | the card that lists the others |
| `claim` | one statement in a card's header |
| `term` | a name used in an atom that has no card |

Arrows come from three places.

- **From a link.** `[[level-crossing]] {relation: uses}` in a card is an arrow
  of the type `uses` from that card. A link with no relation is a `mentions`.
- **From an atom.** A claim with the atom
  `requires(strange-loop, level-crossing)` is an arrow of the type `requires`
  from `strange-loop` to `level-crossing`. It carries the belief of its claim,
  and a denied atom is an arrow believed at less than one half.
- **From the header itself.** A card `claims` each of its claims, and a claim
  is `under` its north star.

One fact can be written twice, once as a link and once as an atom. The link
says the cards are connected and how. The atom says a card answers for it with
a probability, and it can be denied. They are kept as two arrow types with the
same name and different signatures.

The same graph drawn at both levels. Below are a few of its nodes. Above are
the types those nodes have. This card is a node below, and it holds the level
above.

```d2
direction: up

graph: The graph {
  strange-loop: strange-loop
  level-crossing: level-crossing
  claim: "strange-loop#2"
  star: levels-not-cycles
  question: building-a-loop-in-hmd
  knowledge-graph: knowledge-graph {
    style.bold: true
  }

  strange-loop -> claim: claims
  claim -> star: under
  strange-loop -> level-crossing: requires
  knowledge-graph -> question: answers
  knowledge-graph -> strange-loop: distinguished-from
}

schema: The schema {
  concept
  claim
  star
  question
  meta
  term

  concept -> claim: claims
  claim -> star: under
  concept -> concept: "13 link and atom types"
  concept -> question: answers
  concept -> term: "requires, produces, has"
  meta -> concept: "uses, distinguished-from"
  meta -> question: answers
  meta -> claim: claims
}

graph.strange-loop -> schema.concept: is typed by {
  style.stroke-dash: 3
}
graph.knowledge-graph -> schema.meta: is typed by {
  style.stroke-dash: 3
}
graph.knowledge-graph -> schema: holds in its header
schema -> graph: checks {
  style.stroke-dash: 3
}
```

### Arrows are functions

Typing the nodes says what kinds of thing exist. That is a statement about
domains, and it catches little: nearly any card is a valid `concept`.

Typing the arrows says which mappings between domains are allowed. An arrow
type is a signature, a set of node types it may start from and a set it may
end at. `answers` runs from a `concept` to a `question` and nowhere else.
`under` runs from a `claim` to a `star`. A card that declares it `answers`
another concept is a well-typed node making an ill-typed arrow, and only the
check of the arrow sees it.

This is why the arrow type is the important one. The content of the vault is
on its arrows: the relations, and the atoms of the claims. A check that stops
at the nodes validates the nouns and leaves every sentence unread.

The signatures are loose today. Most of them say `concept` to `concept`,
because that is what the cards do. They are a record of present usage that can
be tightened, and each tightening is a claim about the vault that the check
will then hold it to.

### Rules of inference

The header holds a second kind of rule, under `rules`. A signature says what
an arrow may be. A rule of inference says what follows from the arrows there
are: from `is-a(x, y)` and `is-a(y, z)` it gives `is-a(x, z)`. Each rule is a
list of atoms with variables, and either the atom they give or the statement
that they clash.

With these rules the atoms are a small [[formal-system]]: the asserted atoms
are its axioms, and an atom the rules yield is a theorem, carried with the
claims it rests on. The rules apply to arrows written as atoms only. A link
is not a premise, since no claim answers for it.

The rules are not applied by a query. The script `scripts/derive.py` reads
them from this header, which leaves this card in the position it already had
with the schema: it represents the rules, and what applies them is outside
the vault.

### The loop

The schema is not in the query that checks the graph. It is in the header of
this card, and the query reads it from there. The check then runs over every
node and arrow, this card's among them.

So the card is in two positions at once.

- At the level of the cards it is one node among some four hundred, with
  links, claims and a type, checked like any other.
- At the level of the rules it is where the rules are. Its header says what a
  node and an arrow may be.

Each position constrains the other. This card links to
[[building-a-loop-in-hmd]] with the relation `answers`. That link is valid
only because the entry for `answers` in this card's header lists `meta` among
the types it may start from. Remove `meta` from that entry, and the check,
reading its rules from this card, reports this card. The card can make itself
invalid by editing itself.

### What kind of loop this is

The vault has a test for a strange loop and this card has to pass it or say
that it does not. Taking the test from the cards that state it:

- **A representation the system can operate on.** Present. The schema is data
  in a header, a query reads it as a typed value, and the thing represented is
  the graph that contains the header.
- **A crossing of levels.** Present in a limited sense. A rule about cards is
  read off a card, and the card is judged by the rule. Whether that is the
  crossing the vault means has to be shown and is not shown by saying so. The
  claim is carried at a credence of one half.
- **The interpretation changes the mechanism.** Absent. The check reports and
  stops. Nothing in the vault turns a violation into a change of the schema. A
  person edits the header.

By that test this is static reflection: a system that can state and check some
of its own properties. It is a [[self-reference]] and it is not a
[[strange-loop]]. A check that runs every day and changes nothing is not even
a [[feedback-loop]].

It becomes a candidate when the third condition is met: an agent reads the
violations, proposes a change to the schema as a revision, the revision passes
the gates of [[render-integrate-execute]], and the next check runs under the
schema it helped to change. Then the graph's description of itself takes part
in changing what it describes.

Two limits hold even then.

- The schema checks the form of arrows and not whether its own signatures are
  the right ones. A signature that is wrong and consistently followed raises
  nothing.
- The query language that runs the check is outside the vault. This card
  represents the rules of the graph and not the interpreter that applies them,
  which is the distinction [[object-and-meta-level]] draws between
  representing a construction inside and executing it from outside.

## Relations

- It is a case of [[self-reference]] {relation: example-of}: a description that is among the things it describes.
- It is not a [[strange-loop]] {relation: distinguished-from}: its check changes nothing.
- It depends on [[object-and-meta-level]] {relation: uses}: the cards are one level and the rules about cards another, and this card is on both.
- Its claim to a [[level-crossing]] {relation: uses} is the open part, held at one half.
- It corresponds to a [[reflective-formal-system]] {relation: analogous-to}: a system carrying an encoding of its own syntax, with nothing fed back.
- It corresponds to a [[metacircular-interpreter]] {relation: analogous-to} in what it lacks: the rules are represented, the interpreter is not.
- Its rules of inference make the atoms a [[formal-system]] {relation: uses}, of which it holds the rules and not the engine.
- An arrow type is a signature, as a [[typed-transformation]] {relation: analogous-to} is for an operation.
- It would be changed by [[revision]] {relation: uses} through the gates of [[render-integrate-execute]] {relation: uses}, and by nothing else.
- It bears on the question [[building-a-loop-in-hmd]] {relation: answers}: it is the smallest structure in the vault on which the loop could close.
- It bears on the question [[ai-support-in-hql]] {relation: answers}: header data read as declared types is what the check needs from the language.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[object-and-meta-level]].** The distinction between a system and
  statements about it. This card is a statement about the graph that the graph
  contains.
- **[[reflective-formal-system]].** A theory that carries an encoding of its
  own syntax. It is the static case, and so is this.
- **[[observer-card-experiment]].** A card that retrieves itself as a value.
  This card is retrieved as a value by the check, and what is retrieved is a
  schema and not only a text.
- **[[typed-transformation]].** An operation given a signature. An arrow type
  is the same idea applied to a relation.

## Queries

The arrow types, read from this card's own header:

```hql
from kg import schema_of

cards
| filter(c => c.name == "knowledge-graph")
| flat_map(c => match schema_of(c) { some(s) => s.arrows, _ => [] })
| map(t => fmt("{} ({}): {} -> {}", t.type, t.written, t.from | join(" | "), t.to | join(" | ")))
```

The arrows that start from this card, each with the verdict of the schema this
card holds:

```hql
from kg import Schema, Node, node_of, claim_nodes, header_arrows, atom_arrows, link_arrow, schema_of, check

nodes = [cards | map(node_of) | List, cards | flat_map(claim_nodes) | List] | concat_all
here = cards | filter(c => c.name == "knowledge-graph") | List
arrows = [
    here | flat_map(header_arrows) | List,
    here | flat_map(atom_arrows) | List,
    graph.edges | filter(e => e.source == "knowledge-graph") | sort(by = e => e.target) | map(link_arrow) | List,
] | concat_all

fn verdicts(s: Schema) : List<String> =
    arrows | map(a => fmt("{} -> {} ({}): {}", a.from, a.to, a.type, check(s, nodes, a).unwrap_or("conforms")))

here | flat_map(c => match schema_of(c) { some(s) => verdicts(s), _ => [] })
```

The whole graph against the schema is the query `schema-check.hql` in the
query library.

